# JavaScript / JSX

## Style

- Prefer modern ES modules (`import`/`export`); match the project’s module
  system when it differs
- Prefer `const`/`let` — never `var`; prefer `const` unless reassignment is
  required
- Prefer template literals over string concatenation
- Use strict equality (`===` / `!==`)
- Document with JSDoc on public APIs, modules, classes, functions, and
  exported symbols (`@param`, `@returns`, `@throws` when relevant)
- Prefer explicit types in JSDoc when TypeScript isn’t in play
- Match the repo’s ESLint/Prettier config; do not fight it
- Use the project’s existing package manager; do not introduce another
  without an explicit ask

## Docs & comments

- File-level JSDoc when a module’s ownership isn’t obvious from the path
- Inline/`//` comments follow `code-comments`

```code-javascript
/**
 * Load a user by ID from the directory service.
 *
 * @param {string} userId - Stable external identifier for the user.
 * @returns {Promise<User>} The matching user record.
 * @throws {UserNotFoundError} If no user exists for `userId`.
 */
export async function fetchUser(userId) {
  ...
}
```

## Structure

- One public function/component should do one job; extract helpers when it
  orchestrates several steps
- Prefer several small units over one function that mixes I/O, parsing,
  business rules, and UI
- Prefer a sensible folder layout by responsibility over a single large file
- Default: one primary export per module; keep extra exports only when tightly
  coupled
- Be reasonable: don’t create empty layers for their own sake

## JSX

- Prefer function components over class components
- Keep components focused; pull hooks, handlers, and presentational pieces
  out when the file grows
- Props are part of the public contract — document them
- Prefer composition over deep prop drilling when a clearer structure exists
- Keep JSX readable: extract noisy conditionals and mapped lists
- Do not place assignments or other side effects inside JSX expressions
- Use stable, meaningful keys for collections; never random values
- Use semantic elements; preserve keyboard access, labels, and alt text

## Robustness

- Throw specific `Error` subclasses (or project error types); never empty
  `catch`; prefer `cause` when rethrowing
- Validate untrusted input at boundaries
- Prefer `async`/`await`; always handle rejections
- Prefer structured logging over `console.log` when a logger exists
- Never use `eval`, `new Function`, or string-built executable code
- Escape or sanitize untrusted content before raw HTML insertion
- When a test runner exists (`jest`, `vitest`, etc.), ship tests in the same
  change as new/changed behavior

## Refactors

Keep structural refactors separate from behavior changes unless the user
explicitly asks for both.
